WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.819
Here are two hundred providers, and a secret.

00:00:02.859 --> 00:00:07.218
Six of them truly differ from
the reference: three worse,

00:00:07.258 --> 00:00:11.699
three better. The other 194
are, in truth, identical.

00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:16.657
And here are their ratios, sorted from
highest to lowest. A league table.

00:00:16.697 --> 00:00:18.476
Can you pick out the six?

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.965
Take the three at the top and the three at
the bottom, and you would be right about two.

00:00:24.005 --> 00:00:29.568
The other four are identical providers, small
ones, having a lucky or an unlucky year.

00:00:29.608 --> 00:00:34.097
A league table cannot tell luck from
difference. We need a picture that can.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:37.888
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:37.928 --> 00:00:39.665
Today: the funnel.

00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:45.319
Start with Dune's pile from
Episode 13: what luck allows,

00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:49.010
for a clinic like the
reference with Dune's patients.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.960
Divide every count by the 4.8 expected,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.206
and the pile sits on the
ratio scale, centred on 1.

00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:02.864
Keep the stretch that holds
95% of it: from 0.38 to 1.62.

00:01:02.904 --> 00:01:07.947
Stand that band upright. A ratio
inside it is unremarkable for Dune.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.915
How wide the band is depends on
how much a clinic can tell us.

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:14.619
Call that its precision. Dune's is 10.

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:23.073
Birch has more to go on: a precision of
39, and a narrower band, from 0.69 to 1.31.

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:29.168
Now do that at every
precision, and join the edges.

00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:34.124
Wide on the left, where clinics tell us
little. Narrow on the right. A funnel.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:40.947
Place the cast. Dune, Cedar and Birch
sit inside. Alder, at 1.62, is outside.

00:01:40.987 --> 00:01:45.611
Outside the funnel means flagged,
by last episode's bell-curve test.

00:01:45.651 --> 00:01:48.334
It is the same calculation, drawn.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:53.474
And Elm, far to the left. At Elm's
precision the lower edge is below zero.

00:01:53.514 --> 00:01:59.526
No count, not even none, could flag so
small a clinic as better than the reference.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.971
Now the two hundred from the start.

00:02:05.011 --> 00:02:11.447
Of the 194 identical providers, 13
fall outside the funnel, by luck alone.

00:02:11.487 --> 00:02:15.910
That is what a 5% line means.
Draw the crowd again and again,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.154
and on average 5% of them land outside.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.759
And the six who truly differ?
The funnel catches three.

00:02:22.799 --> 00:02:26.771
It misses both of the small
ones, with 37 patients each.

00:02:26.811 --> 00:02:30.784
And it misses a better-than-reference
provider with 105:

00:02:30.824 --> 00:02:34.541
a low count is harder to
prove than a high one.

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:39.549
So a dot outside is not a verdict,
and a dot inside is not an acquittal.

00:02:39.589 --> 00:02:44.041
Inside means consistent with
the reference, not equal to it.

00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:49.476
Why put precision along the bottom,
and not the number of patients?

00:02:49.516 --> 00:02:52.813
Take two providers with 100 patients each.

00:02:52.853 --> 00:02:58.137
One treats patients whose risk
is near 5%; the other, near 40%.

00:02:58.177 --> 00:03:04.522
Their precisions are 5 and 67. The same
size, at opposite ends of the funnel.

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:11.054
In our crowd, providers of about a hundred
patients have precisions from 19 to 74.

00:03:12.633 --> 00:03:16.346
One companion picture. Sort
the providers by ratio,

00:03:16.386 --> 00:03:20.124
and give each one its
interval from Episode 13.

00:03:20.164 --> 00:03:23.142
The funnel shows how much
each provider can tell us.

00:03:23.182 --> 00:03:27.767
The caterpillar shows the order, and
how little the order can be trusted.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:33.148
A footnote on the funnel. With the exact
test, each provider has limits of its own,

00:03:33.188 --> 00:03:37.807
stepping between whole counts. The
smooth curve is a guide to them.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.206
One last turn of a knob. Until now, all
but six of our providers were identical.

00:03:46.246 --> 00:03:50.267
Let them truly differ a
little, as real providers do.

00:03:50.307 --> 00:03:53.281
Now a quarter of the dots
are outside the funnel.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.112
Is each of them an outlier?

00:03:55.152 --> 00:03:59.396
Or is the funnel too narrow for a
world in which everyone differs?

00:03:59.436 --> 00:04:02.966
Hold that question. It
has an episode of its own.

00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:06.029
Let's gather it up.

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:11.789
Up the side, the ratio. Along the bottom,
precision: how much a provider can tell us.

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:16.482
The funnel is what luck allows at each
precision. Outside it means flagged.

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:20.862
And dots on the left are not worse
providers. They are quieter ones.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.655
Every picture in this act has leaned on
something we have not yet questioned:

00:04:25.695 --> 00:04:27.317
the risk model itself.

00:04:27.357 --> 00:04:29.542
Next time: can we trust it?

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:33.772
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
